    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That's a good start. Can you go a bit deeper into why vertical scaling might cause those \"performance bottlenecks\" you mentioned? Like, what are the actual technical limits we'd hit if we just kept adding RAM or CPU to a single instance?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Vertical scaling hits hard physical ceiling limits, such as the maximum RAM capacity or clock speeds available in a single server, around which point performance degrades instantly rather than scaling smoothly. Furthermore, as that single node approaches these hardware plateaus, it becomes a single point of failure where a minor hardware malfunction can take down the entire entire application. Relying on this approach often leads to increasing costs linearly for only marginal, diminishing returns in processing power. Therefore, transitioning to horizontal scaling is crucial to build a resilient architecture that can grow seamlessly without hitting these rigid hardware constraints.",
      "source": "target",
